WEBVTT

00:00:03.000 --> 00:00:05.538
Before sunrise near Casselton,
North Dakota,

00:00:05.538 --> 00:00:08.942
a tractor plants rows straighter than any
hand could steer,

00:00:08.942 --> 00:00:12.230
guided by satellites twenty thousand
kilometers overhead.

00:00:14.767 --> 00:00:20.187
Then the guidance drifts. Is it the Sun,
an adversary, or the machine?

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.889
Those satellites are flown around the
clock by Space Force and Air

00:00:25.889 --> 00:00:29.660
Force Reserve operators at Schriever
Space Force Base, Colorado.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:34.686
A presidential order calls their signals a
largely invisible utility:

00:00:34.686 --> 00:00:40.120
for the power grid, for every mode of
transportation, for precision agriculture.

00:00:40.500 --> 00:00:42.621
Losing GPS would cost the U.S.

00:00:42.621 --> 00:00:47.640
economy about a billion dollars a day, and
more during spring planting.

00:00:48.500 --> 00:00:50.737
Food has always been strategy.

00:00:50.737 --> 00:00:55.136
In 1972, huge, unanticipated Soviet grain
orders drove U.S.

00:00:55.136 --> 00:01:01.100
wheat prices sharply higher. The early
signals were there, but weren't acted on.

00:01:01.433 --> 00:01:06.218
Within a few years, NASA, USDA and NOAA
were estimating wheat harvests

00:01:06.218 --> 00:01:10.933
from orbit, and spotted a Soviet shortfall
two months before harvest.

00:01:10.967 --> 00:01:13.823
When war closed Black Sea ports in 2022,

00:01:13.823 --> 00:01:17.037
satellites tracked fields no one could
visit.

00:01:17.200 --> 00:01:21.140
North Dakota grows more than half of
America's spring wheat.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:26.216
Food and agriculture is one of sixteen
critical infrastructure sectors.

00:01:26.216 --> 00:01:28.640
Farm security is national security.

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.057
And the signal is contested.

00:01:31.057 --> 00:01:35.102
In May 2024, the strongest solar storm in
two decades knocked

00:01:35.102 --> 00:01:38.550
out farmers' GPS guidance in the middle of
planting.

00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:43.859
On Finland's eastern border, tractor
guidance swung two meters off the line,

00:01:43.859 --> 00:01:47.587
from interference apparently coming across
the border from Russia.

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:52.038
In the Black Sea, ships' GPS placed them
at an airport.

00:01:52.038 --> 00:01:54.420
From the cab, it all looks the same.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:02:00.838
Same symptom. Different cause. Different
response:

00:02:00.838 --> 00:02:05.750
halt the fleet, report the interference,
or service one tractor.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:10.036
Getting it right means fusing evidence
that arrives on different clocks,

00:02:10.036 --> 00:02:13.400
from different owners, and saying honestly
how sure you are.

00:02:15.933 --> 00:02:19.592
No single agency, company or university
sees all of it.

00:02:19.592 --> 00:02:25.113
NOAA warns when the Sun erupts. Commercial
satellites geolocate jammers from orbit.

00:02:25.700 --> 00:02:28.563
University researchers turn a NASA
mission's

00:02:28.563 --> 00:02:30.841
GPS echoes into soil-moisture maps.

00:02:30.841 --> 00:02:33.639
And the Space Force's
Unified Data Library,

00:02:33.639 --> 00:02:36.762
developed by Bluestaq, brings that data
together

00:02:36.762 --> 00:02:39.040
for operators, analysts and allies.

00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:43.175
Food security is national security.

00:02:43.175 --> 00:02:48.887
When the guidance drifts, someone has to
decide. Give them evidence worth trusting.

